# 1. FIRST: INSPECT THE EXISTING PROJECT

Before writing code, inspect the existing repository thoroughly.

Determine:

- Whether Next.js is already configured.
- Which Next.js version and routing approach are being used.
- Whether TypeScript is enabled.
- Which styling system is installed.
- Whether Tailwind CSS is configured.
- Whether shadcn/ui or another component library is present.
- Whether authentication already exists.
- Whether backend API endpoints are available.
- How environment variables are configured.
- Whether existing components, layouts, and design tokens can be reused.

Inspect the existing project structure before making architectural decisions.

IMPORTANT:

- Do not overwrite working backend integration.
- Do not delete existing features without justification.
- Do not introduce duplicate dependencies unnecessarily.
- Do not replace real API calls with hardcoded frontend data.
- Preserve working authentication and authorization.
- Reuse existing components when they are appropriate.
- If backend APIs are incomplete, create clean API integration boundaries and explicitly identify missing endpoints.

After the inspection, implement the frontend systematically.

Do not stop after producing a design plan. Implement the actual application.

