# 3. DESIGN DIRECTION: UNIVERSITY EDITORIAL MINIMALISM

Use the provided Khazar University events-page screenshot as the primary visual reference.

Extract the visual principles rather than copying the page.

The design should combine:

- Official university website aesthetics.
- Black-and-white editorial design.
- Large typography and strong hierarchy.
- Minimal borders and restrained separators.
- Generous whitespace.
- Carefully aligned content grids.
- Rectangular or subtly rounded components.
- High-quality typography.
- Professional tables and data visualization.
- Understated interactive states.
- Functional, accessible navigation.

The finished product should look as though a university's central placement office commissioned a professional design agency to build it.

The website must feel institutional, trustworthy, sophisticated, and functional.

Avoid the visual language of a generic AI startup landing page.

## 3.1 Color palette

Use a restrained monochrome palette.

Define these design tokens:

- Primary background: #FFFFFF
- Main text: #111111
- Secondary text: #666666
- Muted text: #858585
- Subtle background: #F5F5F3
- Secondary surface: #FAFAF9
- Border: #E4E4E1
- Strong border: #222222
- Primary button background: #111111
- Primary button text: #FFFFFF
- Secondary button background: #FFFFFF
- Secondary button text: #111111
- Focus indicator: a clearly visible high-contrast outline

Use black for primary actions and white for the dominant page background.

Use gray to communicate hierarchy without making secondary information difficult to read.

Do not introduce purple, blue, pink, orange, or green accent colors throughout the interface.

Functional statuses may use subtle neutral treatments, but they must remain understandable without relying on color alone.

For example:

- Applied: neutral outlined badge.
- Shortlisted: stronger text with a subtle gray background.
- Interview Scheduled: dark badge with white text.
- Selected: bold, clearly labeled status.
- Rejected: muted text and a restrained border.

Always display the status label in addition to any visual treatment.

## 3.2 Typography

Use a refined sans-serif font system.

Preferred options include Geist, Inter, or an equivalent locally available font.

Use:

- Large, confident headings.
- Clear differences between page titles and section headings.
- Compact typography for metadata.
- Readable body text.
- Consistent line heights.
- Restrained letter spacing.
- Monospaced typography for selected identifiers, dates, or numerical metadata where appropriate.

Do not make every heading bold.

Use font weight, size, spacing, and alignment to establish hierarchy.

### Typography scale

Suggested desktop sizes:

- Main landing-page heading: 76–104px.
- Main dashboard page title: 44–60px.
- Section heading: 26–36px.
- Card or table-section heading: 18–22px.
- Body text: 14–16px.
- Secondary metadata: 12–13px.

These are starting points, not rigid values. Use responsive typography and ensure text does not overflow.

On mobile, scale headings down appropriately using fluid typography such as clamp().

## 3.3 Layout and spacing

Use a consistent spacing system based on multiples of 4px.

The main content should generally have:

- Maximum content width of approximately 1440px.
- Centered content containers.
- Generous desktop margins.
- Consistent horizontal alignment.
- Clear separation between page sections.
- Comfortable mobile padding.

Do not place every component inside a card.

Use a combination of:

- Open editorial sections.
- Horizontal separators.
- White content surfaces.
- Minimal bordered panels.
- Structured tables.
- Full-width headings.
- Asymmetric layouts where they improve visual storytelling.

Cards should be used when they represent a meaningful, independent piece of information.

Avoid excessive rounded corners. Prefer square corners or a small radius of approximately 2–6px.

Avoid oversized shadows.

